1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,063
You've probably seen Astra build games and beautiful 3D scenes.

2
00:00:08,166 --> 00:00:10,790
I've been using it on something harder to show in

3
00:00:10,848 --> 00:00:14,447
a short clip: a deployment across Azure,

4
00:00:14,598 --> 00:00:17,176
Linux, Windows, and a database.

5
00:00:18,279 --> 00:00:20,845
It involved networking, certificates,

6
00:00:20,995 --> 00:00:23,016
and real problems along the way.

7
00:00:23,968 --> 00:00:27,973
That experience changed how much of a job I'm willing

8
00:00:28,031 --> 00:00:31,677
to give AI. I've also been using it for everyday

9
00:00:31,735 --> 00:00:35,206
writing, where the prose has felt more natural.

10
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:40,291
So I looked at my experience alongside the documentation and

11
00:00:40,442 --> 00:00:44,192
early public reports. Six things stand out.

12
00:00:44,970 --> 00:00:47,350
The question connecting them is simple:

13
00:00:48,465 --> 00:00:52,052
what happens when Astra has an actual job to carry,

14
00:00:52,377 --> 00:00:57,381
inside the systems and tools we already use?

15
00:00:57,381 --> 00:01:02,942
First, complex deployment. The business application ran on Linux.

16
00:01:03,256 --> 00:01:05,729
The reporting engine needed Windows.

17
00:01:06,495 --> 00:01:09,455
I wanted a request to travel from the application to

18
00:01:09,571 --> 00:01:14,006
a Windows service, produce a PDF using the database,

19
00:01:14,169 --> 00:01:16,514
and return it to the business system.

20
00:01:17,466 --> 00:01:21,228
Existing connections and workflows still had to work.

21
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:24,525
That was the real job behind the words,

22
00:01:24,641 --> 00:01:28,961
Windows Worker. The work included Azure resources,

23
00:01:29,239 --> 00:01:33,872
private networking, a new subnet attached to an existing NAT

24
00:01:33,976 --> 00:01:36,762
Gateway, Windows service installation,

25
00:01:37,146 --> 00:01:39,073
and SQL certificate trust.

26
00:01:39,886 --> 00:01:43,206
Astra advanced those steps within my authorization.

27
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:48,442
I decided the scope and approved changes that could affect

28
00:01:48,593 --> 00:01:53,980
services. But I wasn't switching into every tool to perform

29
00:01:54,061 --> 00:02:00,400
the implementation myself. That made the experience feel very different.

30
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,826
Azure rejected an early network rule.

31
00:02:03,546 --> 00:02:07,087
Database connectivity brought certificate checks.

32
00:02:08,225 --> 00:02:10,280
Later, the PDF existed,

33
00:02:10,884 --> 00:02:12,927
but saving the attachment failed.

34
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:15,598
The work continued through diagnosis,

35
00:02:15,749 --> 00:02:17,978
read-back, and retry checks,

36
00:02:18,164 --> 00:02:20,091
until the chain worked in Dev.

37
00:02:20,788 --> 00:02:23,771
What impressed me was connecting infrastructure,

38
00:02:23,922 --> 00:02:28,996
code, troubleshooting, and verification inside one task.

39
00:02:29,692 --> 00:02:33,419
The value was in carrying those pieces together.

40
00:02:33,419 --> 00:02:36,484
Second, the tools are becoming more connected.

41
00:02:37,471 --> 00:02:41,047
OpenAI's showcases include a Blender house model,

42
00:02:41,291 --> 00:02:44,425
an Unreal scene, and a racing game.

43
00:02:45,261 --> 00:02:48,199
Another compares clinic routes in Google Maps.

44
00:02:48,919 --> 00:02:50,753
These are official demonstrations,

45
00:02:50,823 --> 00:02:55,606
not my reproductions. I look at them for the handoffs:

46
00:02:56,721 --> 00:02:59,739
a result in one place becoming the input to the

47
00:02:59,809 --> 00:03:05,544
next step. Matt Shumer describes browser work on newsletters,

48
00:03:05,857 --> 00:03:09,154
inboxes, and advertising interfaces.

49
00:03:09,572 --> 00:03:13,880
A flight-game developer reports using Godot and Blender,

50
00:03:14,298 --> 00:03:16,097
with feedback along the way.

51
00:03:16,515 --> 00:03:18,953
The useful combination includes code,

52
00:03:19,185 --> 00:03:21,972
applications, and tool connections.

53
00:03:22,738 --> 00:03:26,372
Research, create, inspect, continue.

54
00:03:27,266 --> 00:03:30,900
Each handoff the agent handles is one less point where

55
00:03:31,004 --> 00:03:32,920
I have to take the task back.

56
00:03:33,686 --> 00:03:37,320
Access and available tools still matter.

57
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:43,682
Third, long tasks. Codex documents experimental context management with notes

58
00:03:43,729 --> 00:03:47,908
and searchable history. It is currently off by default.

59
00:03:48,663 --> 00:03:50,567
The idea is straightforward:

60
00:03:51,043 --> 00:03:53,551
when a job grows beyond the current context,

61
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,931
earlier details remain retrievable.

62
00:03:57,045 --> 00:04:01,480
Imagine a refactor where a dependency cannot be upgraded.

63
00:04:02,595 --> 00:04:08,202
Hours later, the reason for that decision still matters.

64
00:04:08,203 --> 00:04:12,766
In that example, a useful record includes the migration that

65
00:04:12,882 --> 00:04:15,796
failed and what the tests revealed.

66
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:19,859
Recovering it could prevent another trip down the same dead

67
00:04:19,929 --> 00:04:23,993
end. This is an illustration of the documented feature.

68
00:04:25,108 --> 00:04:28,974
It matters because long jobs accumulate discoveries.

69
00:04:30,088 --> 00:04:34,117
I want the context invested early in a task to

70
00:04:34,198 --> 00:04:37,600
keep helping as the work moves on.

71
00:04:37,599 --> 00:04:40,827
Fourth, initiative has to work with direction.

72
00:04:41,512 --> 00:04:43,590
I want the model to keep going,

73
00:04:44,089 --> 00:04:47,003
while understanding a condition I add along the way.

74
00:04:47,688 --> 00:04:50,811
If I say, keep this in the test environment,

75
00:04:51,183 --> 00:04:53,052
that changes the next steps.

76
00:04:53,853 --> 00:04:56,442
My view is that upgrading the model is also a

77
00:04:56,489 --> 00:05:00,169
good time to review our old skills.

78
00:05:00,169 --> 00:05:04,174
OpenAI recommends auditing skills and instruction files.

79
00:05:05,045 --> 00:05:09,283
Ambiguous or conflicting rules can make Astra stop too early.

80
00:05:10,455 --> 00:05:13,219
I would replace ask me after every step with a

81
00:05:13,277 --> 00:05:16,051
clear goal, acceptance criteria,

82
00:05:16,237 --> 00:05:18,362
and meaningful decision points.

83
00:05:19,441 --> 00:05:26,001
Scope still matters: one developer reported unwanted infrastructure and premature

84
00:05:26,048 --> 00:05:29,972
implementation. Better instructions help,

85
00:05:30,343 --> 00:05:35,092
but the model remains responsible for understanding the job.

86
00:05:35,092 --> 00:05:37,936
Fifth, the writing feels more natural.

87
00:05:38,772 --> 00:05:41,443
Recent drafts connect ideas more easily,

88
00:05:41,652 --> 00:05:43,927
and the prose is more pleasant to read.

89
00:05:44,902 --> 00:05:47,317
I often begin with scattered thoughts,

90
00:05:47,608 --> 00:05:51,253
project experiences, and a point I'm still finding.

91
00:05:52,402 --> 00:05:55,015
When those become a coherent article,

92
00:05:55,224 --> 00:05:57,302
I can focus on what I mean to say.

93
00:05:58,114 --> 00:06:01,748
That's the improvement I feel in my writing workflow.

94
00:06:01,748 --> 00:06:05,335
A concrete experience leads into an observation.

95
00:06:06,253 --> 00:06:10,212
The next paragraph picks up the question it leaves behind.

96
00:06:10,885 --> 00:06:13,648
I like the writing we're producing together more.

97
00:06:14,577 --> 00:06:16,179
This is my experience,

98
00:06:16,295 --> 00:06:20,556
with our existing context and preferences contributing too.

99
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:24,167
I still tell it who the reader is and what

100
00:06:24,225 --> 00:06:29,124
tone I want. Those choices help the expression fit the

101
00:06:29,182 --> 00:06:36,985
piece. Sixth, cost. The flight-game developer reported about three hours

102
00:06:37,043 --> 00:06:41,118
and forty-five percent of a Pro plan's weekly allowance.

103
00:06:42,163 --> 00:06:45,367
Other users describe running out before finishing.

104
00:06:45,843 --> 00:06:47,515
Those are individual reports,

105
00:06:47,549 --> 00:06:49,639
but they make the tradeoff visible.

106
00:06:50,243 --> 00:06:53,308
I would use a lighter model for a small edit,

107
00:06:53,912 --> 00:06:58,277
and consider Astra when the work spans systems,

108
00:06:58,788 --> 00:07:04,535
troubleshooting, and verification. These experiences change what I'm willing to

109
00:07:04,616 --> 00:07:08,656
delegate. I feel more confident giving Astra long,

110
00:07:08,714 --> 00:07:12,139
complex tasks across products and systems,

111
00:07:12,290 --> 00:07:14,740
within a clear goal and scope.

112
00:07:15,460 --> 00:07:17,991
Greater autonomy brings new questions:

113
00:07:18,525 --> 00:07:22,089
how do I verify the result and trace the important

114
00:07:22,136 --> 00:07:26,826
decisions? Verification and auditing need more attention.

115
00:07:27,778 --> 00:07:29,659
So does the higher price.

116
00:07:30,599 --> 00:07:34,756
I'm honestly tempted to pay for another high-allowance subscription.

117
00:07:35,557 --> 00:07:38,982
Seeing useful work get done makes me want more room

118
00:07:39,063 --> 00:07:43,545
to use it. Those questions haven't taken away my excitement.

119
00:07:44,357 --> 00:07:47,387
I want the work I still have to watch closely

120
00:07:47,457 --> 00:07:49,849
to become work I can trust it with.

121
00:07:50,801 --> 00:07:53,819
AI is still finding ways to surprise me.

122
00:07:54,423 --> 00:07:56,850
I'm looking forward to it getting better,

123
00:07:57,094 --> 00:08:00,356
and to feeling those improvements in real work.
